#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088596} :activity/id #uuid "d11823b5-bd6a-59d3-b59c-fa3609b4e9e5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722 :topic/id #uuid "614ee6b8-9a8a-566b-9824-c4103b2e358e" :topic/math-academy-id 806} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088925 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIf we make a table of values and plot them, we can see why this is a circle. Let's label each point $A-H.$\n\n|  | $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,C\\,$ | $\\,D\\,$ | $\\,E\\,$ | $\\,F\\,$ | $\\,G\\,$ | $\\,H\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\theta\\,$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$ |\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $\\,y\\,$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\n![](images/d5/d50cf22b3e7429924ce0039f4daa2191234e628173e6f5be789a872ffa520346.png)\n\nThe $\\theta$ domain $0\\leq \\theta<2\\pi$ ensures that we go around the entire circle. If we were to cut the $\\theta$ domain in half (i.e., $0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi$), we would get a semicircle.\n\nFor a general circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin $O,$ the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThis parametrization starts at the point $(r,0)$ (corresponding the $\\theta = 0$) and traverses the circle in the counterclockwise direction. This is the most common parameterization of a circle and is the one that we'll mostly use.\n\nAnother set of parametric equations for a circle of radius $r$ centered at $O$ is\n\n$$\nx=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=r\\cos\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5b85ab1-1c6e-58ee-b07a-850e9af41e85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3501 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fc22d83d-d595-5978-9c2c-096f6a937738" :step/math-academy-id 19851 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087554 :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403321}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "7aca0d0e-a717-59ae-a74e-18f19112edd3" :step/math-academy-id 19852 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399503}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"} :step/id #uuid "e9a6fb2b-64e1-50e9-8d33-f068fe5ae1fd" :step/math-academy-id 19853 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087612 :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397836}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "83ab4754-dd9f-5b1e-83f6-b4b93fe07188" :step/math-academy-id 25057 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087616 :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526386} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526385}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"} :step/id #uuid "003cb75d-2a77-51c2-bc65-749bdef698bb" :step/math-academy-id 25242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088960 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFor example, the parametric equations of a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(1,-2)$ are\n\n$$\nx= 2\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,-2),$ the rightmost point on the curve.\n\n![](images/69/692b62bc09342cae8b8be19f135ecb9e5b98460ad65ee13bfdddf39a7e1a5b6b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "801f608f-f3d3-5612-9c1f-45d78532f743" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4284 :tutorial/title "The Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"} :step/id #uuid "ac1e138c-9132-571a-8812-16394c6aaaed" :step/math-academy-id 25994 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088961 :tutorial/content "To convert the parametric equations of a circle to a Cartesian equation, we can eliminate the parameter $\\theta$ using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the circle defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=5\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2 = 25.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae768e3d-4836-50d3-95b7-d5e142a470fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4285 :tutorial/title "Converting the Parametric Equations of a Circle to Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "45680d1f-e317-530f-84b4-835f674c3aee" :step/math-academy-id 25995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088664}}] :activity/title "Parametric Equations of Circles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087894}]}
